to me and he's like,

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.698
who let us be superheroes?

00:05:25.698 --> 00:05:27.510
And then we just started dying.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:29.190
And that was kind of
like, the start of just

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:32.550
this beautiful like,
relationship and just like,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.410
friendship, you know, with Noah.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.960
Couldn't be more thankful for it.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:37.793
- Yeah.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.460
I would love to just
acknowledge something.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.380
So what Quintessa just said, you know,

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:45.300
when they come in with just that kind of

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.690
really cool humility of like,
who let us be superheroes?

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:49.523
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:49.523 --> 00:05:51.810
- That's the kind of like,
effervescence that came on set

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.070
with with these guys.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.320
It was really just so cool to see like,

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.960
to watch these guys come
on set with that kind of

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.350
appreciation and gratitude,
but also that type of like,

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.360
holy, we're doing it.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:04.260
- Yeah.
- It's amazing.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.370
- Oh my god, and y'all like,

00:06:05.370 --> 00:06:06.210
I have a six and eight year old.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.280
They get so excited. It's a
cool thing to play superhero.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.270
Like, all the ki- they literally
like, they get so excited.

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:13.620
You're like supernatural to the-

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:15.090
even though you're really just human.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.370
Like they- it's a cool part to play like,

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.237
for a lot of kids out
there too, it's awesome.

00:06:19.237 --> 00:06:22.080
- We showed the movie at my
house there the other week

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:24.990
and my boys and my stock with
my boys and all their friends

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.490
just went way up.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:27.780
- It's like the first
time you've done something

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:28.770
amazing for them?

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.320
- Truly. They were just so blown away.

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.635
- That's so funny.

00:06:32.635 --> 00:06:33.968
Not Bond, not-

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:35.796
- Not Thomas Crown Affair.

00:06:35.796 --> 00:06:38.178
None of the things, yeah.

00:06:38.178 --> 00:06:39.105
- No.

00:06:39.105 --> 00:06:41.815
- Well done, Pierce, you
finally got there, buddy.

00:06:41.815 --> 00:06:45.150
- I could see their faces
just beaming with joy for me

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.200
and for the whole movie.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.220
They just loved it.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.080
- Well wait, let's talk
about Dr. Fate for a minute

00:06:49.080 --> 00:06:53.490
because what I found amazing
is the whole time I kept going,

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:55.890
how does one see when that's your costume?

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:58.860
Like, your costume has no-

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.420
- You don't see anything
- No eyes.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.253
- Is it really, you don't see-

00:07:01.253 --> 00:07:04.440
- No, it's just like your
putting a bucket on your head.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:06.480
I mean it looks gorgeous and I did-

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.940
I mean Jaume Colett-Serra-

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.030
- He can't see.

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.510
Lucky you don't, like,
suffer from claustrophobia

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:13.380
or anything like that.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:15.120
- No, but- well, there's two helmets,

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:18.090
there's the kind of ornate
beautiful helmet that I carry

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.250
and then there's this large bucket helmet

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.590
that I would put on my head, very briefly,

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:23.640
because I couldn't see anything.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:24.473
I put it on.

00:07:24.473 --> 00:07:25.306
- Oh my god.

00:07:26.411 --> 00:07:27.290
- It's ridiculous.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.720
It was- there was a point in
the movie where I transform

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.460
and it's this kind of can
and I have to reach down

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.230
and get this bag.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.650
So, action, put it on and it's...

00:07:40.533 --> 00:07:43.050
Sorry, sorry. Let's do it again.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.360
- I love it, it was like
you're Doctor Fate and like,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.160
I guess fate is in someone else's hands

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:49.130
'cause you can't see. I was like-

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:50.820
It was amazing.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:53.370
Okay, so Quintessa,
you're one of the first

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:56.700
non-binary actors to play a
superhero in the DC Universe.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.624
So what does that mean to you?
It's gotta feel pretty cool.

00:07:59.624 --> 00:08:03.480
- I mean, I think, you know,
maybe I'm one of the first,

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.950
you know, I'm out.

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:05.783
So that's-
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:08:05.783 --> 00:08:07.320
- You know, that's one thing.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:09.120
You know, there could be
people who are questioning

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:11.760
or you know, still figuring, you know,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.460
what that means to them
and their gender identity.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.143
But for me, I think it just-
it's an incredibly, like-

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:24.540
I don't like, take it lightly.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.870
I think because as a young kid,

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:29.670
I never would've thought that like someone

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:33.420
who's non-binary or trans could be open

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:36.420
enough with who they are and
like, showcase who they are,

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:37.860
you know, globally,

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.680
and get, you know, far.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.470
And be able to do the things
that they've always dreamt of.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:47.130
And you know, just fully
just embrace who they are.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.670
So I think being here
and like, being out and,

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.620
you know, discussing these
things and even, you know,

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.230
you asking that question is so important

00:08:55.230 --> 00:08:57.810
because there could be
a a young trans person

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:01.350
or someone who's just
questioning who needs to see

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.540
themselves represented in some sort of way

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:05.940
just to know that they can do anything

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.530
that they dreamt of, so-

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:08.600
- Absolutely.

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:09.433
- Woo!
- Inclusivity.

00:09:09.433 --> 00:09:12.197
(audience cheering)

00:09:12.197 --> 00:09:13.030
Oh my gosh.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:16.380
Well, Aldis, so what did the
role of Hawkman mean to you?

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:18.783
You're so cool. It was
like, and I loved it.

00:09:19.947 --> 00:09:22.380
And your chemistry is so hilarious,

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.674
how y'all are fighting

00:09:23.674 --> 00:09:24.900
- Oh, we go at it.
like a lot of the time.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:25.733
- Yeah, yeah.

00:09:25.733 --> 00:09:28.380
No, in terms of like what
it meant to me, it's-

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.920
first of all, definitely
I am a superhero fan.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.080
I got into the game because of DC.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:36.840
I just wanted to do jobs to go buy toys

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.670
and comics, you know?

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:42.420
So for me to be able to present Hawkman,

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.450
this titular figure, in
this way, for the first time

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:48.300
on a film with this amazing cast,

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:52.262
with the biggest God darn superstar.

00:09:52.262 --> 00:09:54.595
(cheering)

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.800
- It doesn't get better than that, but...

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.520
for me on the back end,
even more important,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.350
you know, much to Qs point,

00:10:04.350 --> 00:10:07.470
being able to be a vessel in
the progressive conversation

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.020
that allows little black and
brown kids to see themselves

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.900
and see that impossible is possible.

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.910
They can never go back to thinking,

00:10:14.910 --> 00:10:18.390
oh, I can't be a part of
that, or that's imaginary.

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:22.080
They now, the standard is
I'm supposed to be there.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:22.913
- [Quintessa] That's right.

00:10:22.913 --> 00:10:24.222
- And that is never gonna change.

00:10:24.222 --> 00:10:26.190
- That's good.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:28.723
- You know what, I say this
all the time on the show,

00:10:28.723 --> 00:10:31.950
but inclusivity is important for everyone,

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:33.840
not just those who don't feel
like they're represented.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:37.320
It's important for my
kids to see every culture,

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.510
to know every- it's
important for every kid

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.510
to see everything.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:41.944
- [Quintessa] Yeah.
- [Aldis] Yeah.

00:10:41.944 --> 00:10:43.381
- Like, it's just as important.

00:10:43.381 --> 00:10:47.160
Because your- my kids would be missing out

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.780
on that opportunity to learn something.

00:10:48.780 --> 00:10:49.829
- [Aldis] Yeah.

00:10:49.829 --> 00:10:50.662
- That's important.

00:10:50.662 --> 00:10:53.670
- What it does is, cohesively,
it allows us as a culture

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:56.820
to move forward towards that
progressive goal together

00:10:56.820 --> 00:10:58.950
as a unit, which is how it should be.

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.783
- Yeah. I agree, I agree.

